  "self_critique": "Overall assessment\n\nThe run found a small gain over baseline (0.6018 \u2192 0.6040), but did not convert that gain into a clear scientific conclusion. It converged after only three follow-ups, all based on weakly supported objective or ensemble changes rather than isolating what made node_001 successful.\n\nWhat the harness/policy did suboptimally\n\n- Node_001 changed too many dimensions simultaneously: architecture, BPR formulation, recency weighting, and scheduled regularization. Calling this a \u201cmatched full-factorial search\u201d is not enough unless all cells and their scores are retained. The accepted result cannot be attributed to any component or interaction.\n- Subsequent proposals did not exploit the most valuable next step: ablation of node_001. Instead, they introduced LambdaLoss, asymmetric temporal pairs, and an ensemble, adding more uncertainty.\n- The journal repeatedly says the parent learning curve was unavailable. Missing train/validation curves prevented diagnosis of underfitting, overfitting, training instability, or premature stopping. The policy should treat these artifacts as mandatory.\n- Node_002\u2019s premise cites \u201ccontext-stratified BPR reached 0.605212,\u201d which is inconsistent with the reported accepted primary of 0.603990/0.6040. Internal-selection metrics, fold metrics, and final primary appear to have been conflated.\n- The score reporting is confusing: node_004 is shown as 0.6045 but rejected against a 0.6040 parent, while best_primary remains 0.603990. If rejection was due to uncertainty, paired significance, or unrounded scores, the journal should state that explicitly.\n- The ensemble proposal was poorly timed. Ensembling can raise the metric, but it does not explain the node_001 gain and may compound validation-selection overfit.\n- Stopping as \u201cconverged\u201d after three rejected, structurally different ideas was too aggressive. This demonstrates failure of those ideas, not exhaustion of the local neighborhood.\n- Baseline sigma=0.0001 looks unusually precise. Seed count, paired evaluation procedure, and uncertainty for every candidate should have been recorded consistently.\n\nWhat I would change in the scaffold\n\n- Require every trial to preserve learning curves, best epoch, train loss, validation metric, seed, and exact configuration.\n- Separate search score, validation primary, and final acceptance score with unambiguous names.\n- After a multi-factor winner, automatically schedule one-factor dropouts and additions before permitting new objectives.\n- Use paired multi-seed comparisons and report mean delta, standard error, and acceptance threshold. Log why a numerically higher score was rejected.\n- Require hypotheses to identify one controlled intervention and a falsifiable mechanism. Avoid unsupported point forecasts such as \u201c+0.0015.\u201d\n- Add a minimum local-search budget around an accepted node; do not declare convergence merely after several unrelated failures.\n- Prefer cheap diagnostic experiments before ensembles or complex ranking-loss modifications.\n\nWhat the next run should try first\n\nReproduce node_001 across several fixed seeds, then run exact ablations against the same baseline:\n\n1. Baseline FM with only the architecture change.\n2. Baseline plus only hybrid BPR.\n3. Baseline plus only recency weighting.\n4. Baseline plus only scheduled regularization.\n5. The full node_001 configuration.\n6. Drop-one-component variants of the full configuration.\n\nUse identical data splits, negative sampling, training budget, and early-stopping rules. Record paired deltas and curves. If budget permits only one experiment initially, test the full node_001 configuration versus the baseline across multiple shared seeds. The immediate priority is determining whether the 0.0022 gain is reproducible and which component causes it\u2014not introducing another ranking objective."
